From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: RJ Skerry-Ryan <rryan@alum.mit.edu>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 07:55:16 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: Add CPU hotplug for Apple spin-table CPUs

Apple SoCs have no PSCI: the boot loader starts the secondary CPUs and
hands them to the kernel through a spin table, so CPUs can never go
offline. The PMGR can stop and start individual cores, but on the T6020
family a core that was powered down on its own never fetches its reset
vector when it is started again; only the first start after its whole
cluster was power cycled works.

Add spin-table cpu_operations for CPUs whose device tree node points at
the PMGR CPU controls (apple,cpu-ctrl and apple,cpu-impl-reg), selected
in place of the plain spin-table operations. An offline CPU parks in the
kernel instead of powering down through the PMGR:

- cpu_die makes WFI power the core down with state retention (CYC_OVRD
  WFI mode 3 without the retention disable, the state the cpuidle "CPU
  PD" state uses), cleans and invalidates the L1 data cache by set/way
  and WFIs until it is given a command. A retention WFI keeps only sp
  and pc, so the loop keeps the callee-saved registers on the stack and
  writes nothing to memory after the clean. Its interrupt modes are left
  alone so that a fast IPI wakes it; every wake-up acknowledges one;
- cpu_kill waits for the core to report that it parked, and closes the
  holding pen;
- cpu_boot writes the pen release, gives the core the resume command and
  sends it a fast IPI, then waits for the core to take the command. The
  command is withdrawn if it does not, so a core cannot wake up into the
  kernel later. The core restores CYC_OVRD, turns its MMU off through the
  identity map with the kexec soft restart code and enters the holding
  pen as on first boot.

Suspend to RAM power cycles every CPU complex, so the secondaries it
takes offline power down through the PMGR instead: cpu_die cleans the L1
data cache, sets the PMGR stop request, masks IRQ/FIQ wakeups and WFIs
without state retention, and cpu_kill waits for the core status to report
it powered down. On resume cpu_boot checks the core's RVBAR, rewriting it
if it is unlocked and no longer points where it did at offline, writes
the pen release and sets the core's PMGR alive bit and start request. The
core comes out of reset at its RVBAR, where the boot loader's resident
secondary entry path jumps to the spin table release address the kernel
wrote at boot, the holding pen.

A core that does not park or power down, or does not come online after
its start, is not started again. First boot is unchanged. The boot CPU
never gets these operations. CPUs whose controls are missing stay online.
Early boot failures and crash stops still park in the generic code.

Also export the PMGR power-down tail as apple_cpu_deep_sleep() for the
system suspend path.

Signed-off-by: RJ Skerry-Ryan <rryan@alum.mit.edu>
[dylan: carried from aurora-silicon/linux b9e9232e973e; one tool trailer of the original message is not reproduced here, see the upstream commit for the full text]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
